    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, History 2022 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "hist-2022-hl-lm-s1-q3b", topicId: "hist-lm-irl-5", conceptId: "consider-objective-source-reasons",
    section: "1", questionRef: "2022 HL Later Modern Section 1 Q3(b)",
    stem: "Case study: The Coleraine University controversy",
    questionText: "Would you consider that document B is an objective source? Give reasons for your answer, referring to the document.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Candidates may argue for and/or against the view that doc B is objective. Evaluate the answer on the quality of the points made to support the given opinion.", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "The candidate may attempt to define what he/she understands by objectivity, e.g. to give a fair account of events/ to try to take all sides into consideration/ freedom from bias/ not one-sided or personal/ exclusion of personal feelings or beliefs, etc.", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "McCormack looks back on events from her perspective as a trade unionist and human rights activist, therefore she cannot be taken as an objective source.", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "McCormack expresses strong opinions in doc B. She labels the Lockwood Report a “blatantly political decision” and calls the leading Unionist citizens who secretly supported the Report “modern-day Lundys” motivated by “sectarian purposes”. She favours the civil rights movement, describing their actions as “peaceful and determined” and saying that the NI state had repeatedly tried to “humiliate” the people of Derry. These are emotive rather than objective terms.", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "McCormack tries to be objective by giving a range of views in her account of events. She describes the sense of “outrage” among the citizens of Derry while also giving information about the “Seven Faceless Men” and “maverick Unionist”, Desmond Boal.", marks: null, openList: true },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "Candidates may argue that because Inez McCormack came from a Protestant background and opposed the Lockwood decision that doc B is therefore objective. She shows her objectivity by not following the hardline Unionist point-of-view.", marks: null, openList: true },
    ],
